schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: The Funding Landscape
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    A map of web3 and ReFi-native funding mechanisms for local node builders,
    organized around one axis: prospective funding (grants, quadratic funding)
    that pays before the work happens, versus retrospective funding (RetroPGF /
    Retro Funding, hypercerts) that pays after impact is shown. Also surveys
    direct grants, streaming/recurring funding, yield-based ("evergreen")
    funding, and conviction voting, and closes on Sybil/collusion risk in QF
    and the case for blending funding sources so no single funder sets a
    node's direction.
  audience: local node organizers evaluating funding mechanisms
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      Quadratic funding rewards breadth but is structurally vulnerable to
      Sybil attacks and collusion; every fix (e.g. identity verification via
      Gitcoin Passport/Human Passport) trades away some decentralization for
      integrity.
    - >-
      Retro funding rewards track record, which favors established nodes over
      brand-new ones with no visible output yet.
    - >-
      External/grant funding "often means external priorities entering your
      community space" — the article's own defense is blending sources, not
      avoiding funding.
  related_concepts:
    - quadratic-funding
    - retroactive-public-goods-funding
    - hypercerts
  related_resources:
    - octant
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/funding-landscape.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

